<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.11.1: Graphing Linear Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01037</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.11.1: Graphing Linear Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>902c6273-a0a5-42af-b5ec-994e6bd81985</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (10 minutes)</title>

<para>This warm up recalls how to graph linear functions and which lines are parallel or perpendicular. It also introduces how one linear function can be shifted to another.</para>
<para>Later in the lesson, students will graph linear functions only using knowledge of transformations to prepare them for later units when they graph other functions with transformations.</para>
<para>For students who can benefit from a more accessible model, use geoboards and rubber bands. Use one color rubber band for the \(x\)- and \(y\)-axes. Use a different color for each line graphed. Work with students to count spots on the geoboard and discuss the graphs and their relationships.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of 2. Allow students to compare their graphs with their partners and discuss the relationships of the lines. </para>
<title>Student Activity</title>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the following lines given in slope-intercept form: (Students were provided access to Desmos)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=3x +4\)</item>
  <item>\(y=3x - 2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/763bf9e9b6700dc554c82e9addacab94c734ce91"/></media></figure></para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the relationship between the two lines you graphed in question 1?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> They are parallel lines.</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>When you examine the equations of the lines, what do you notice is the same about the equations?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>The slopes of the lines are equal.</item>
  <item>The numbers in front of the variables are the same.</item>
</list>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>When you examine the equations of the lines, what do you notice is different about the equations?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>The constant term is different in each equation.</item>
  <item>The number that is added to the equation is different.</item>
  <item>The \(y\)-intercepts for each equation is different.</item>
</list>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>When you examine the graph of the lines, how many vertical spaces are between the two lines?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> There are 6 vertical spaces between the two lines.</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>On your coordinate grid, use the slope and \(y\)-intercept of each equation to graph each equation. Use a different color for each equation: (Students were provided access to Desmos)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=3x-2\)</item>
  <item>\(y=-\frac13 x-3\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6b0a2dd1c0662d5beda17d819a3671482baf7e41"/></media></figure></para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>When you examine the graph of the lines in question 6, explain if the lines appear to be perpendicular.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>No, the lines don't look like they intersect at right angles - so they don't look like they are perpendicular. (Check the settings of your graph, this is incorrect.)</item>
  <item>Yes, the lines look like they intersect at right angles - so they appear perpendicular. (This is the correct answer.)</item>
</list>
<para>Remember to identify perpendicular lines algebraically, examine their slopes. If the slope values are opposite reciprocals, then the lines intersect at right angles and are perpendicular.</para>


<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share their interpretations of the given equations and the new equations they wrote. Then, discuss with students:</para>
<list>
  <item>“Do you notice any other relationships between pairs of lines?”</item>
  <item>“How many vertical spaces are between lines \(a\) and \(b\)?”</item>
</list></section>
